From: Nikolajs Agafonovs <nikolajs.agafonovs@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:22:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] The desktop's wallpaper: icons drawn on the root window's
 background pixmap

The desktop window covered the root window with the background colour,
so a wallpaper never showed.  xfiles -d now reads the root window's
_XROOTPMAP_ID (the Esetroot convention: the program that sets the
background publishes its pixmap there) and draws the icons on that
picture: on a 32-bit visual under everything with OverReverse, where the
layers stay transparent; on a server without one (TinyX), where the
layers are opaque, each cleared area of the icon layer, the label pixmap
and the canvas gets the piece of wallpaper it covers on the screen (the
icon layer is redrawn when the scroll offset it was cut for changes).
Labels get a one-pixel dark shadow there, legible on light pictures.  A
change of the property reloads it; a pixmap that is gone is caught and
leaves the colour background, and one smaller than the screen repeats
(JWM's 1x1 for a colour).
---
 widget.c | 215 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 205 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

